\chapter{A course lab adapted for learnlog: prgi26, Laboration 0}
\label{prgi26-lab0}

This appendix is a worked example of adapting an existing course lab
to [[learnlog]].
The lab is the first, optional lab of the KTH course DD1317
(\foreignlanguage{swedish}{Programmeringsteknik}, course instance
prgi26): install Python, install an editor, write a Hello World
program, and remix two small programs before handing one in for peer
review.
The adaptation makes three changes.
It keeps one editor, VSCodium, instead of offering a choice of three;
it installs and wires [[learnlog]] \emph{before} the first program is
run; and it ends with the student looking back at their own recorded
runs.
The student-facing text stays in Swedish, the language of the course,
and this chapter documents in English why the lab is shaped as it is.

The lab is tangled from this chapter into [[prgi26-lab0.md]] for the
same reason the onboarding page in \cref{sec:students-page} is
tangled from that chapter: the commands a student types must match
what the program actually does, and tangling from the manual is the
only way to keep the two from drifting apart.
The Markdown carries the frontmatter that the [[canvaslms]] tool
uses to find the assignment, so a teacher with a source checkout
publishes it from the tangled file:
\begin{minted}{bash}
make -C doc prgi26-lab0.html
canvaslms --no-cache assignments edit --html -c prgi26 \
  -f doc/prgi26-lab0.html
\end{minted}
The assignment in the course is a FeedbackFruits assignment, an
external tool reached through LTI, and the two details of this
command exist to leave that wiring alone.
[[assignments edit]] sends only the name and the description, plus
whichever optional fields the frontmatter lists, so a frontmatter
with nothing but [[name]] and [[regex]] cannot touch the submission
type or the tool link; and the [[.html]] target converts the Markdown
with [[pandoc]] beforehand, which is the route that was verified on a
sandbox copy of such an assignment before the first publish.
[[--no-cache]] matters after a rename: the tool's object cache can
hold the old and the new name of one assignment and then refuse an
ambiguous match.
Both tangled files are build artefacts and, like [[students.md]],
are not tracked in the repository.

This lab and the onboarding page now describe two different ways of
installing [[learnlog]], and the difference is deliberate.
The onboarding page installs [[learnlog]] with [[pipx]], which puts
the command on the [[PATH]] of every shell, and lets
[[learnlog init python]] create the project environment.
This lab creates the project environment first and installs
[[learnlog]] inside it.
The whole lab takes place in VSCodium's integrated terminal, which
the editor keeps inside the project environment, so a beginner meets
exactly one environment and one Python; the price is that the
[[learnlog]] command exists only in terminals that use that
environment, which the lab states twice.

\section{The object of learning and the shape of the lab}

The object of learning is to write, run and change a Python program in
an environment of one's own, with the work recorded.
The critical aspects a beginner has to discern are that a program is
a file, that running it produces output in a terminal, and that the
same word [[python]] can name different interpreters, of which only
one is wired to record.
Everything else in the lab---download pages, installer dialogs,
platform differences---is scaffolding that should stay in the
background.

The lab therefore keeps the course folder invariant from the moment it
is created and rotates one tool at a time into focus: Python, the
editor, Git, [[learnlog]], and finally the program itself.
This is the same separation pattern that orders the tutorial catalog
in \cref{tut:tutorials}, where the recorded project is the invariant
and each tutorial varies one part of the CLI.
The order is not the order of the original lab, which installed
Python, then let the student choose an editor, then wrote the program.
Moving [[learnlog]] ahead of the first program is what makes the
first run and the first record simultaneous, and that simultaneity is
what the closing step depends on: there is a history to look back at
only if recording started before the history did.

Each section below pairs the analysis of one step with the Markdown
that implements it, so the two can be read side by side.
The root chunk assembles the sections in the order the student meets
them.

