\section{Looking back}

The last step turns the lab's own history into its final exercise.
[[learnlog play]] fuses what the earlier steps separated: the file,
the run, the output and the change since the previous run appear in
one record, and the student can move between records.
This is the step the whole sequencing was for, and it is the reason
[[learnlog]] records review commands at all: looking back is part of
the work, not an audit of it.

The prompt before the student starts browsing is a try-first prompt.
It asks which change made the biggest difference, which the student
must answer from memory before the record answers it for them; the
question does not say what to look at in the record, only what to
compare, so the aspects that matter---which run, what it printed
before and after---are the student's to discern.
The comparison of expectation with record is the contrast that makes
the record worth having.

<<lab0: looking back>>=
## Titta tillbaka

Nu har du kört och ändrat program ett antal gånger, och learnlog har
sparat varje körning. Innan du tittar: vilken av dina ändringar tror
du gjorde störst skillnad i vad programmet skrev ut? Bestäm dig för
ett svar.

Skriv sedan i terminalen

```
learnlog play
```

Du får upp en visare som visar en körning i taget: när den gjordes,
vilket program som kördes, vad det skrev ut och hur koden ändrats
sedan körningen innan. Bläddra mellan körningarna med piltangenterna
vänster och höger, rulla i en lång körning med upp och ner, och
avsluta med `q`.

Leta upp körningen där din ändring dök upp och jämför vad programmet
skrev ut före och efter. Stämde det med vad du trodde?

@

\section{Handing in}

The submission paragraph is the original's, unchanged: the remixed
program is handed in on Canvas and reviewed by two classmates, which
practises the mechanics the graded labs will use.
The lab does not ask the student to share their [[learnlog]] record;
the record stays on their machine, as the introduction promised, and
sharing is a later lab's step.

